#### 2.8 Collect, report and use local data on a standardized set of basic process indicators for care and newborn outcomes
Health workers gather at the Gautampuri Mother and Child Welfare Centre, to monitor outcomes of routine immunization sessions, Delhi, India. © WHO / Christine McNab
Whether paper-based, digital or a combination, local facility data is vital not only for providing essential newborn care but also for evaluating the quality of care, supporting decision-making in the facility and health system, and using quality improvement to create change. Essential Newborn Care Course second edition orients participants to the many uses of data in the cross-cutting module on data collection and use. Quality Improvement Templates initiate Plan-Do-Study-Act (PDSA) cycles and the use of data to evaluate tests of change and demonstrate sustained improvement. Through educational activities, health workers become engaged in collecting complete and accurate data and involved in determining what data to collect.
ENCC supports efforts to develop and utilize important perinatal datasets, including:
1. civil and vital registration systems;
2. HMIS indicators (utilizing uniform definitions/datasets);
3. ENAP indicators;
4. quality-of-care indicators; and
5. facility-level and regional perinatal databases (e.g. ANA, CLAP).
